  "introduction": [
    "Tao Te Ching",
    "A close English translation of the eighty-one chapters",
    "This translation follows the received eighty-one-chapter text. It aims to keep the Chinese images, repetitions, and tensions visible in English, even where a smoother paraphrase might be easier. The source text is ancient and has variant readings; punctuation and some phrases are interpretive. “The ten thousand things” means all things; “power” renders de; “not acting” renders wu wei and means action that does not force or overmanage."
  ],
